.TH LEAP 1
.SH NAME
leap \- a side-scrolling platformer written entirely in the shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh /lib/tiles/leap.sh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Leap
is a platformer: run, jump, take the coins, do not fall in a pit or walk
into a walker.
Arrow keys or
.BR wasd ;
Space,
.B w
or Up to jump;
.B Esc
or
.B q
stops.
.PP
.IR Warren (1)
showed that a game could be written in the shell.
This one is the parts
.I warren
did not need:
.TP
gravity and a jump
A velocity in pixels, a constant added to it each frame, a landing that
snaps to the top of whatever was hit, and a jump allowed only from the
ground \- which is what stops it being a flap.
.TP
a level wider than the window
The level surface is 512 logical pixels across and the screen is 256;
.B view
copies the window of it the camera is over, and the camera keeps the
player a third in and stops at both ends.
.TP
things that move on their own
A walker is the string
.BR "x,y,direction" ,
a swarm is a list of them, and the list is rebuilt each frame rather
than mutated \- because the shell has no way to assign to one element of
a list.
.PP
That last one turns on what the shell already has:
.BR ${index} ,
.B ${split}
and
.B ${join}
from
.IR sh-std (1),
and
.IR expr (1)'s
reverse-Polish arithmetic.
The shell has no subscript syntax \-
.B $l(2)
is the list followed by a
.B 2
\- and
.B $#l
counts but does not index, so those three are the whole of how a shell
program holds a table.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/leap.sh
the game
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/leap.map
the level, legend and all
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/demo.chr
the tile art
.SH SOURCE
.B /lib/tiles/leap.sh
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR warren (1),
.IR sh-tiles (1),
.IR tiles (2),
.IR sh-std (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
What a frame costs, measured with
.BR ms ,
scrolling a 512-wide level into a 256-wide window and walking N walkers:
.IP
.EX
0 walkers      2500 us a frame
4 walkers      6100
12 walkers    14800
24 walkers    26200
40 walkers    43100
.EE
.PP
About a millisecond a walker, and a 60Hz budget is 16666 microseconds.
So a platformer or a maze runs at full rate with room to spare, and a
formation of forty attackers \- which is what
.IR starling (1)
puts on the screen \- would run at about twenty-three frames a second.
That is the honest answer to whether every game can be written in the
shell: the ones with a handful of things moving, yes; the ones with
dozens, not at speed.
.PP
The cost is round trips and not drawing.
A walker is eight or nine
.B ${expr}
and
.B ${tiles}
substitutions, at about 26 microseconds each, and the drawing it does is
one
.BR put .
.SH BUGS
There is one level and it does not end; running off the right just
stops.
.PP
The player is one tile and does not animate.
.PP
A walker turns at a ledge and at a wall and does nothing else \- it does
not fall, and two of them will walk through each other.
.PP
Movement is three pixels a frame with no acceleration.
.IR Sprocket (1)
has momentum and a jump whose height follows the button; both are
possible here and both are more
.B ${expr}
per frame, which is the budget above.
